## 💻 C++ Code (With Line-by-Line Comments)

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>  // for hash map
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> twoSum(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        // Hash map to store value -> index
        unordered_map<int, int> mp;

        // Loop through all elements
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            int x = nums[i];               // current number
            int complement = target - x;   // the other number we need

            // Check if the complement is already in the map
            if (mp.find(complement) != mp.end()) {
                // Found it! return the stored index and current index
                return {mp[complement], i};
            }

            // Otherwise, store this number with its index
            mp[x] = i;
        }

        // In case no solution found (problem guarantees one solution)
        return {};
    }
};
```

---

## 📝 Key Explanations
- `unordered_map<int, int>` → stores pairs like `{value → index}`.  
- `mp.find(key)` → checks if key exists in O(1) average time.  
- `mp[complement]` → retrieves the stored index.  
- Insert step: `mp[x] = i` → means "store the number x at index i".  

---

⚡ Complexity:
- **Time:** O(n) (each number checked once, map operations O(1) on average).  
- **Space:** O(n) (map can store up to n elements in worst case).  

